# Paths whose review is reserved to a specific role, rather than any reviewer.
#
# GitHub enforces these entries only under the "Require review from Code Owners"
# branch-protection rule; without it they merely auto-request the reviewer.
#
# The paths below share a shape: the automation around them checks that declarations
# agree with one another, never that a declaration should have been made at all.
# Weakening one leaves every check green, and review is what is left.

# Supply-chain controls — the cooldown settings, the lockfiles they produce, the pin
# lockfiles, and the two bypass ledgers. A narrowed window leaves no trace in the
# resulting lockfile, so `npm-cooldown-audit.yaml`, which reports a bypass but never
# blocks it, cannot see one either.
**/.npmrc                             @Tomy-ch
**/package-lock.json                  @Tomy-ch
**/pnpm-lock.yaml                     @Tomy-ch
**/pnpm-workspace.yaml                @Tomy-ch
.github/actions-pin.toml              @Tomy-ch
.github/go-cooldown-bypass.toml       @Tomy-ch
.github/tool-cooldown-bypass.toml     @Tomy-ch
docker/images-pin.toml                @Tomy-ch

# The Go cooldown gate reads the `go.mod` diff and covers direct requirements only,
# which leaves indirect modules to review.
go.mod                                @Tomy-ch
go.sum                                @Tomy-ch

# The version SSOT. `tool-cooldown.yaml` covers the CLI tools declared here; the
# language runtimes are exempt by the file's own declaration, and `make sync-versions`
# carries them into go.mod, the Dockerfile FROM tags and the READMEs.
mise.toml                             @Tomy-ch

# The `harden-runner` allowlist SSOT — `make egress-apply` writes each entry out to
# every workflow that carries the job.
.github/egress.toml                   @Tomy-ch

# What the Codacy linters are given to read. An exclusion added here takes code out of the
# analysis and leaves the job green.
.codacy.yaml                          @Tomy-ch
